module dev.slatelang.slate

import sh.sysl.gc
import sh.sysl.libuv
import sh.sysl.miniz
import sh.sysl.parsing.*
import sh.sysl.sqlite
import sysl.buf.{Buf, buf}
import sysl.container.{Map, map}
import sysl.rand.{Rng, rng}
#if feature_lmdb
import sh.sysl.lmdb
#endif
#if feature_http2
import sh.sysl.nghttp2.*
#endif

// The runtime's own state, gathered into one thing a VM owns.
//
// **This file exists so that a second slate VM can exist in the same process.** The state below was
// fifty files' worth of module-level `var`s, which is one interpreter to a process by construction:
// two of them would have written over each other's machine, heap roots, event loop and output. An
// actor on its own thread, and a slate VM a sysl program can hold and embed, are both the same
// missing thing -- a runtime whose state is a *value* somebody owns rather than storage the process
// has. `docs/design/actors.md` is where that is argued; this is the piece of it that had to exist
// first.
//
// **Every field here was a module-level `var` and nothing about its behaviour changed.** The move is
// mechanical and `docs/design/vm-state.md` says how to make the next one: take the `var`, put it in
// the group below that names its subsystem, and write `current().x` where it said `x`.
//
// **A module-level `var` is now refused by `tests_vm_state.sysl` unless it is marked process-wide**,
// which is what stops the fifty files growing back. See that file for the two classes and the count.
struct Vm
    // -- the machine (`vm.sysl`) ---------------------------------------------------------------------

    // The line of execution currently running. **A field and not a local**: `alloc` can collect at any
    // allocation, so what the machine holds has to be reachable from a root function at every instant.
    machine: Machine

    // The lines of execution set aside by `park`. `mark_vm` walks every live one of them, because a
    // suspended call is not running and is not therefore any less alive.
    parked: Buf[Machine]

    // -- the unit being run, and what its modules left behind (`vm.sysl`) ----------------------------

    // The unit being run. **Reached rather than threaded**: a user-supplied `equals` is called from
    // inside a table lookup, which is called from `same`, which knows nothing about units.
    current_unit: Unit

    // What each module exported, in the order the modules ran. A collector root: the closures in it
    // hold their module's scope, and the scope holds whatever it captured.
    module_values: Buf[Value]

    // The scope each module's own statements run in, in the same order.
    //
    // **What a class is looked up in when a message carries an instance of one.** A module's OBJECT
    // is its exports and does not exist until the file has finished; a scope is there from the first
    // statement and holds every name the file binds. `declared_here` in `actor_copy.sysl` is the one
    // reader, and `docs/design/actors.md` is why there is one.
    //
    // **A collector root**, held for the life of the run: a module's scope was already kept alive by
    // any closure it exported, and this keeps it alive for a module that exported none.
    module_scopes: Buf[*EnvObj]

    // -- what the file being run declared around its tests (`vm.sysl`) -------------------------------

    // The tests the entry file declared, in the order they were written. A collector root: a test
    // function is reached from nothing else once its module's scope has been left.
    registered_tests: Buf[TestCase]

    // What the file wrote around its tests, `Null` where it wrote nothing, indexed by `HookSetup` and
    // its three neighbours. A collector root for `registered_tests`' reason.
    hook_fns: Buf[Value]

    // What each of them is called, so a failure in one names the function rather than the test that
    // happened to be standing next to it.
    hook_names: Buf[string]

    // -- the event loop (`event.sysl`) ---------------------------------------------------------------

    // The libuv loop this VM's timers, sockets and requests are on.
    //
    // **A field rather than `default_loop()`, because libuv's default loop is the PROCESS's.** A loop
    // may only be turned by one thread, so a VM running on a thread of its own has to be on a loop of
    // its own -- `new_vm(ceiling, libuv.new_loop().unwrap())` is what an actor writes, and the
    // program's own VM keeps the default loop, which is what every handle in the process was already
    // on.
    uv_loop: &libuv.Loop

    // Everything the loop may still call, and the only thing standing between a pending callback and
    // the sweep.
    pending: Buf[Pending]

    // How many callbacks are still outstanding, which is what decides whether the loop has work.
    // **slate's own count is the authority**; libuv's answer is a spin-check on it.
    outstanding: usize

    // The number the next claim will stamp its slot with. See `Pending.gen`. Never reused, never reset.
    generation: usize

    // The first fault a callback raised, if any. Read once the loop stops.
    loop_fault: Option[Signal]

    // -- promises and the scheduler that resumes what they wake (`async.sysl`) -----------------------

    // The answers waiting to be handed back. **Not `private`: `vm.sysl`'s `Await` queues onto it.**
    ready: Buf[Resumption]

    // How far down `ready` the drain has got. A cursor rather than a shift, so that a resumption which
    // queues more work does not move the entries behind it.
    ready_head: usize

    // Every promise that has failed, whether or not anything was waiting.
    //
    // **Kept until the program ends, because "was this handled?" cannot be answered when it fails.** A
    // promise may be awaited long after it settles -- `val p = risky()` and then `await p` three lines
    // later is the ordinary shape -- so the question is asked once, at the end, of everything that failed.
    rejections: Buf[*PromiseObj]

    // The watches still owed an answer.
    //
    // **A root the collector has to walk**, for `rejections`' reason and more sharply: between the native
    // answering and the promise settling, nothing else holds either of these.
    fault_watches: Buf[FaultWatch]

    // The `all`/`allSettled`/`race`/`any` combinations still owed an answer (`combine.sysl`). A root
    // the collector has to walk, for `fault_watches`' reason.
    combinations: Buf[Combination]

    // -- generators, `await`'s machinery driven by the program (`generator.sysl`) --------------------

    // Every generator a program is holding, and every one it has abandoned half-finished.
    //
    // **Module storage, for the reason the event table is**: a `Machine` holds counted buffers and may
    // not live in the collected heap, so what a `Value` carries is a slot number and the machine lives
    // here. Nothing here needs marking of its own -- the suspended machine is in `parked`, which
    // `mark_vm` already walks, and that is what keeps a half-run generator's locals alive.
    generators: Buf[GenSlot]

    // The number the next claim stamps its slot with. Never reused and never reset within a run.
    gen_stamp: usize

    // -- which file the running line came from (`runtime.sysl`) --------------------------------------

    // Which file the machine is currently running code from, as an index into the unit's sources.
    //
    // **Set by the machine as it enters and leaves a chunk**, because the alternative is threading a
    // file through every one of the 116 places that can raise a fault. A chunk knows which file it was
    // compiled from; the machine knows which chunk it is in; so this is the two facts joined at the
    // one moment anybody needs them.
    current_source: usize

    // -- what the program printed, and what the running test asked (`builtin.sysl`) ------------------

    // Everything the program printed, in order. `run` clears it, which is what lets two programs run
    // one after the other.
    output: Buf[string]

    // Whether `print` also reaches the real descriptor as each call happens. Off by default, because
    // most callers of `run` are asking a question rather than running a program.
    live_output: bool

    // Where a live `print` goes: whatever sink this run was given, so that a captured run's sink sees
    // what its own program printed.
    live_sink: &Fn(string) -> unit

    // Whether the test now running asked to be left out, and the reason it gave. **Written down here
    // rather than matched on the signal**, because a `skip` inside an `async` test travels out of the
    // coroutine as a rejection, which keeps the sentence and loses which variant it was.
    skip_asked: bool
    skip_reason: string

    // -- randomness (`number.sysl`) ------------------------------------------------------------------

    // The generator behind `random()`, and whether it has been seeded yet. Seeded ONCE, on the first
    // draw rather than at startup, so a program that never calls `random()` never asks the kernel.
    draws: Rng
    draws_seeded: bool

    // -- the heap, its roots and the collector's counters (`obj.sysl`) -------------------------------
    //
    // **The heap is a VM's own, and that is what makes two of them possible.** The block is `malloc`ed
    // by `new_vm(ceiling)` and given back by `dispose_vm`; `gc.heap(base, cap)` has always taken the
    // block it works over, so a second VM is a second `base` and nothing else. The collector's root
    // function reaches it through `current()`, which is what used to want module storage.

    // The block this VM's heap is laid over. `malloc`ed once, never resized, freed by `dispose_vm`.
    //
    // **`malloc` rather than `calloc`**: `gc.alloc` zeroes every payload before a caller sees it, so
    // the pages are committed as they are touched rather than all at once -- which is the bargain the
    // BSS array this replaced was making.
    region: *u8

    // How big that block is. The ceiling a `set_heap_limit` is clamped to.
    heap_ceiling: usize

    // The collected heap over `region`. **Rebuilt rather than reused by `reset_heap`**, because the
    // limit may have moved and a free list still describes the old extent.
    heap: gc.Heap

    // How much of the block this program may actually have. `heap_ceiling` is the block and this is
    // the setting, which is what lets a test about running out of memory run out of a small one.
    heap_limit: usize

    // The shadow stack: what the interpreter is holding at a point that may collect, and which no
    // scope refers to. `hold`/`release` and `hold_env`/`release_env` are its whole interface.
    root_values: Buf[Value]
    root_envs: Buf[*EnvObj]

    // How many collections this program has run, which is what makes a claim about *when* slate
    // collects something a test can check rather than something a comment asserts.
    collections: usize

    // How much the live set may grow to before the next collection. Raised after every collection to
    // a multiple of what survived it.
    collect_above: usize

    // Whether the heap has been handed out to the last byte, which is what turns the next allocation
    // into a diagnostic rather than a crash.
    heap_exhausted: bool

    // The emergency objects a full heap is answered with, one per kind. Nothing else refers to them
    // until the moment they are handed out, so nothing else would keep them.
    spare_str: *StrObj
    spare_array: *ArrayObj
    spare_bytes: *BytesObj
    spare_object: *ObjectObj
    spare_closure: *ClosureObj
    spare_env: *EnvObj
    spare_promise: *PromiseObj
    spare_weak_map: *ObjectObj
    spare_weak_ref: *WeakRefObj

    // -- the assets the program imported (`module.sysl`) ---------------------------------------------

    // What every asset a program imports holds, by its resolved path. Filled by `load_program` and
    // emptied by it, so a program's assets never outlive the run that asked for them.
    asset_paths: Buf[string]
    asset_texts: Buf[string]

    // -- compiling (`compile.sysl`) ------------------------------------------------------------------

    // Which file is being compiled, as an index into the unit's sources. `machine`'s own `Frame` holds
    // the run-time twin of this.
    compiling_source: usize

    // Every type any module has declared so far, and where. Filled as each module compiles, which is
    // why no pre-pass is needed.
    known_source: Buf[usize]
    known_name: Buf[string]
    known_pat: Buf[Pattern]
    known_public: Buf[bool]

    // The types the file being compiled may name: its own, and the exported ones it imported.
    visible_names: Buf[string]
    visible_pats: Buf[Pattern]

    // What was wrong with the program, found while compiling rather than while parsing. `run` folds
    // these in with the parser's.
    compile_faults: Report
    compile_fault_srcs: Buf[usize]

    // -- the checker's view of the builtin scope (`check.sysl`) --------------------------------------

    // The scope every program starts in, built once per check. A root nothing else refers to, thrown
    // away when the next program is checked.
    builtin_scope: *EnvObj

    // The builtins' TYPES, kept apart from the walking scope so that a name the program took cannot be
    // mistaken for the builtin a method call means.
    builtin_types: TypeEnv

    // -- running another program, and what this one was started as (`process.sysl`) ------------------

    // Every `run` in flight.
    running: Buf[Run]

    // The executable this program was started as, and the file it was asked to run. Empty where
    // nothing set them: a program under `slate test` or inside the compiler's own suite was not
    // started from a command line of its own.
    program_exec: string
    program_script: string

    // What was on the command line after the program's name, set by the driver before the program
    // starts. A test sets it too, which is what makes `args` reachable from the suite.
    program_args: Buf[string]

    // -- SQLite connections and compiled statements (`sqlite.sysl`) ----------------------------------
    //
    // **The statements are given back FIRST wherever both are.** A statement holds the connection it
    // came from, so clearing the connections while a statement is live would drop a reference that
    // is not the last one and close nothing.
    databases: Buf[Option[&sqlite.Db]]
    statements: Buf[Option[&sqlite.Query]]

    // -- signal watchers (`signals.sysl`) -------------------------------------------------------------

    // Every signal this program is watching. A collector root: this table is the only thing standing
    // between a registered handler and the sweep.
    watches: Buf[Watch]

    // The number the next registration will stamp its slot with. Never reused, never reset within a
    // run.
    watch_generation: usize

    // -- child processes (`spawn.sysl`) and the channel wire (`channel.sysl`) -----------------------

    // Every child this program has started. Nothing is reused: a value the program still holds names
    // this slot for as long as the program runs.
    children: Buf[Child]

    // This program's own end of the channel it was started with, one slot to a program.
    channels: Buf[Channel]

    // The slot `channel()` has already opened, so that asking twice answers the same thing.
    own_channel: Option[usize]

    // -- reading parameters (`define.sysl`, `declare.sysl`) -----------------------------------------

    // Whether each parameter of the definition just read was written `var`, parallel to its
    // `params`. `parse_def` writes it LAST, after its body.
    param_declares: Buf[bool]

    // Whether a `var` parameter is legal where the definition being read stands -- true only in a
    // class's `new`.
    allow_declaring_params: bool

    // -- compiled regular expressions (`regex.sysl`) ---------------------------------------------

    // Every pattern this program has compiled. Never released: a compiled pattern has no `close`
    // and a program cannot ask for one.
    compiled: Buf[Compiled]

    // Keyed by the pattern text and its flags, so an identical pattern compiles once.
    pattern_ids: Map[string, usize]

    // -- function bodies, interned while parsing (`ast.sysl`) --------------------------------------

    // Every function body in the program, and a `Lambda` or a `Def` names one by its index here.
    bodies: Buf[Expr]

    // Whether the body at the same index yields, parallel to `bodies`. Answered by the parser
    // rather than by a walk of the tree.
    body_yields: Buf[bool]

    // -- miniz's scratch state (`gzip.sysl`) --------------------------------------------------------
    //
    // **Heap-allocated rather than an inline array**, so a `Vm` itself stays small: `miniz.DEFLATE_BYTES`
    // for the compressor and `miniz.INFLATE_BYTES` for the decompressor -- each a `sizeof` plus its
    // alignment, around 168 KB and 8 KB -- both sized in `new_vm()` and never resized. Nothing here
    // runs while anything else here is running, so one of each is enough.
    deflate_state: Buf[u8]
    inflate_state: Buf[u8]

    // -- argon2 jobs handed to the thread pool (`argon2.sysl`) --------------------------------------

    // The jobs the pool may still be reading, indexed by the request id that will answer them. A raw
    // pointer keeps nothing alive, so the box is held here for as long as the pool may still read it.
    argon2_jobs: Buf[Option[&Argon2Job]]

    // -- outbound `fetch` calls (`client.sysl`) ------------------------------------------------------
    fetches: Buf[Fetch]

    // -- streaming HTTP parsers (`http_parse.sysl`) --------------------------------------------------
    http_slots: Buf[Slot]

    // -- declared types held as values (`shape.sysl`) ------------------------------------------------
    //
    // **Never given back**: a type declaration runs once and there is no `close` for a shape, so a
    // slot names the same shape for as long as the program runs.
    shape_slots: Buf[ShapeSlot]

    // -- where packages are kept (`packages.sysl`) ---------------------------------------------------
    //
    // A setting rather than a constant, so a test can point it at a fixture. Empty means the ordinary
    // answer: `$SLATE_CACHE`, or `$HOME/.slate/pkg`.
    package_cache_dir: string

    // -- the type parameters a definition is inside (`pattern.sysl`) --------------------------------
    //
    // One parse running at a time, and a region per definition -- `outer[T]` holding `inner[U]`
    // leaves `U` unspellable the moment `inner` ends.
    active_params: ScopedNames

    // -- time zones, interned as they are asked for (`time.sysl`) --------------------------------
    zones: Buf[ZoneEntry]

    // -- TLS state per socket (`tls.sysl`) -----------------------------------------------------------
    tls_slots: Buf[Tls]

    // -- actors (`actor.sysl`) -------------------------------------------------------------------------
    //
    // **Every VM has a mailbox, the program's own included**: the first actor is the program, and what
    // it receives is the answers to its `ask`s.

    // This VM's own mailbox, and the handle another thread wakes it through. `None` for a VM that has
    // neither spawned an actor nor been spawned as one.
    inbox: Option[&sync ActorBox]
    inbox_note: Option[&libuv.Async]

    // How many things only an actor can answer are outstanding here. **The mailbox handle counts
    // towards the loop while this is above zero and is `unref`ed while it is not**, which is what
    // lets a program wait for an actor's answer without waiting for its mailbox for ever.
    actor_waiting: usize

    // The actors this VM spawned, the asks and `done`s it is owed, and the promises its own handlers
    // answered with. **All three are collector roots** -- see `mark_actors`.
    spawned: Buf[Spawned]
    actor_waits: Buf[ActorWait]
    actor_answers: Buf[ActorAnswer]

    // Every handler name a message value in this VM names, interned. A `Value` holds no string.
    message_names: Buf[string]

    // Whether this VM is an actor's rather than the program's, which actor it is, and the instance of
    // the declaration it is running. `me()` is the first two and the drain calls handlers on the
    // third.
    is_actor: bool
    actor_self: usize
    actor_self_gen: usize
    actor_value: Value

    // What it died of, and `""` where it stopped cleanly. Read once, by the broadcast that tells
    // everybody else.
    actor_fault: string

    // Where `actor_fault` was raised: the file's own NAME and its span in that file, carried
    // beside the message for the same reason `PromiseObj.src` is carried beside `PromiseObj.at` --
    // a fault that crosses a thread outlives the source table it was raised against, so what
    // travels has to be a name the OTHER thread can look up rather than an index into a table it
    // never loaded.
    actor_fault_file: string
    actor_fault_at: Span

    // The module whose top level is running while this VM is being loaded for an actor, and `""` at
    // every other moment. **`spawn` is refused while it is set**: an actor's VM runs every module the
    // program imports, so a module that spawned at its own top level would start an actor whose VM
    // ran that module, which would spawn again, without end.
    loading_module: string

    // The names the ENTRY file's top level binds -- its `val`s and `var`s -- filled only where this VM
    // was loaded for an actor. **It is what turns "is not defined" into a sentence that says where to
    // put the value**, the entry file's top level being the one thing an actor does not run.
    entry_effects: Buf[string]

    // -- LMDB's four tables (`lmdb.sysl`), behind the `lmdb` feature ---------------------------------
    //
    // **Gated exactly as `lmdb.sysl` itself is**, `OpenTxn`, `OpenCursor` and `OpenDb` being declared
    // inside that file's own `#if feature_lmdb`.
#if feature_lmdb

    // An environment, a transaction, a database and a cursor, each a number into the table it names.
    envs: Buf[Option[&lmdb.Env]]
    txns: Buf[Option[OpenTxn]]
    cursors: Buf[Option[OpenCursor]]
    dbs: Buf[OpenDb]
#endif

    // -- HTTP/2 sessions and HPACK conversations (`nghttp2.sysl`), behind the `http2` feature --------
    //
    // **Three tables rather than three variants of one**: nothing ever holds two of these at once,
    // so the check is which table a handle is looked up in.
#if feature_http2
    sessions: Buf[Option[&Session]]
    deflaters: Buf[Option[&Deflater]]
    inflaters: Buf[Option[&Inflater]]
#endif

    // -- RESP reader slots (`redis_parse.sysl`), behind the `redis` feature --------------------------
#if feature_redis
    redis_slots: Buf[RedisSlot]
#endif

    // -- the desktop window bridge (`window.sysl`), behind the `webview` feature ---------------------
    //
    // **Gated exactly as `window.sysl` itself is.** `OpenWindow`, `PageBinding` and `PageCall` are
    // declared inside that file's own `#if feature_webview`, so a build without the feature has no
    // such types -- these fields have to be absent along with them rather than merely unused.
#if feature_webview

    // The open windows, and the promise `windowDone` answers about each.
    windows: Buf[OpenWindow]

    // Every `windowBind` registration: which window, and the slate function to call.
    bounds: Buf[PageBinding]

    // Every call a page made that has not been answered, because the handler gave back a promise.
    page_calls: Buf[PageCall]

    // The slot in `event.sysl`'s table that the pump timer lives in, and `None` while no window is
    // open.
    pump_slot: Option[usize]
#endif

// `n` zero bytes, with `len() == n` -- **not `buf_with_capacity`, whose `len()` stays 0** ("A buffer
// that has already been given room for `n` elements" is capacity, not length; `count` starts at
// zero, so its bytes are unread until something pushes into them). miniz's `deflate`/`inflate` check
// `storage.len < N`, so a capacity-only buffer answers `NoRoom` on the first call. Runs once per `Vm`.
private zeroed_scratch(n: usize) -> Buf[u8]
    var b: Buf[u8] = buf()

    for i in 0..<n do b.push(0u8)

    b

// The block a VM's heap is laid over, and the call that gives one back.
//
// **`malloc` rather than a module array, and that is the whole of stage three's first half.** A
// static needs an address and there is one of it; a `malloc`ed block is a value a VM owns, so a
// second VM is a second block. `free` is `dispose_vm`'s, which is the only thing that ever calls it.
extern malloc(bytes: usize) -> *u8
extern free(block: *u8)

// A runtime with nothing in it yet, over a heap of `ceiling` bytes of its own.
//
// **Positional and in field order, because that is the shape sysl gives**, so a field added above is
// added here in the same place or the compiler says which type stopped matching. The groups are the
// struct's groups.
// **`lp` is the loop this VM's handles go on**, and it defaults to the process's own so that every
// call written before there was a second thread means what it always did. A VM that is going to run
// on a thread of its own is given `libuv.new_loop()`, a loop being turnable by one thread at a time.
new_vm(ceiling: usize, lp: &libuv.Loop = libuv.default_loop()) -> Vm
    val block = malloc(ceiling)

    // **Loud rather than degraded.** A null block would be a heap whose first allocation answers null,
    // and `reserve_spares` runs against an empty heap precisely because it may not fail -- so a VM
    // built over nothing would write through null objects rather than report anything.
    if block == null then panic("slate could not take a heap of the size this VM asked for")

    Vm(
        fresh_machine(), buf(),
        new_unit(), buf(), buf(),
        buf(), no_hooks(), no_hook_names(),
        lp, buf(), 0, 0, None,
        buf(), 0, buf(), buf(),
        buf(),
        buf(), 0,
        0,
        buf(), false, no_live_sink, false, "",
        rng(0u64, 0u64), false,
        block, ceiling, gc.heap(block, ceiling), ceiling, buf(), buf(), 0, CollectFloor, false,
        null, null, null, null, null, null, null, null, null,
        buf(), buf(),
        0, buf(), buf(), buf(), buf(), buf(), buf(), report(), buf(),
        null, type_env(),
        buf(), "", "", buf(),
        buf(), buf(),
        buf(), 0,
        buf(), buf(), None,
        buf(), false,
        buf(), map(),
        buf(), buf(),
        zeroed_scratch(miniz.DEFLATE_BYTES), zeroed_scratch(miniz.INFLATE_BYTES),
        buf(),
        buf(),
        buf(),
        buf(),
        "",
        scoped_names(),
        buf(),
        buf(),
        None, None, 0, buf(), buf(), buf(), buf(), false, 0, 0, Null, "", "", point(0), "", buf()
#if feature_lmdb
        , buf(), buf(), buf(), buf()
#endif
#if feature_http2
        , buf(), buf(), buf()
#endif
#if feature_redis
        , buf()
#endif
#if feature_webview
        , buf(), buf(), buf(), None
#endif
        )

// process-wide: THE STORAGE FOR THE PROGRAM'S OWN VM, which is a different thing from the VM a line
// of execution belongs to. Every process has exactly one of these -- the one the driver runs the
// program on -- and it is module storage because it has to outlive everything, including a `main`
// that has returned into a libuv callback. A second VM is an ordinary local somebody owns; see
// `enter` below.
private var main_vm: Vm = new_vm(HeapBytes)

// The one module-level `var` the runtime keeps, and it is a POINTER to the VM in charge OF THIS
// THREAD.
//
// **`@thread_local` is what makes two VMs run at once**, and it is the whole of the mechanism:
// every thread reads its own copy of this pointer, so `current()` answers the VM the line of
// execution belongs to rather than the one the process last entered. `enter`/`leave` below swap
// *this thread's* copy, which is what keeps nesting on one thread working exactly as it did.
//
// **Every copy starts at `null` and `current()` reads that as the program's own VM.** A
// thread-local's copies are stamped from a single image in the object file rather than by code that
// runs per thread, so the initializer has to be a value the compiler can write down and `&main_vm`
// -- the address of a module `var` -- is not one it will take. `null` is, and it says the right
// thing anyway: a thread that has entered nothing belongs to the VM the process runs its program on,
// which is exactly what every thread read before this was per-thread.
@thread_local
private var the_vm: *Vm = null

// The VM this line of execution belongs to.
//
// **A pointer, so that a caller writes through it.** `current().output.push(line)` is the shape, and
// a function that reaches the same subsystem a dozen times takes a pointer to that subsystem once --
// `val vm: *Machine = &current().machine` at the top of `run_frames` is the worked example, and it is
// why converting the instruction loop changed one line rather than a hundred and sixteen.
//
// **The null test is what gives a thread that entered nothing the program's own VM**, and it is here
// rather than in the declaration because a thread-local may not be initialized with an address. It is
// one comparison on a pointer this thread has just loaded.
current() -> *Vm = if the_vm == null then &main_vm else the_vm

// Run what follows on `vm`, and answer the VM that was in charge.
//
// **The previous one travels back through the CALLER rather than through a stack here**, which is
// what keeps this two assignments and no state of its own: a caller that has a `leave` to write has
// the value to write it with, and nesting works because each level is holding its own. The rule is
// that every `enter` is matched by a `leave` on every way out -- including a fault, which is why the
// runtime itself never calls these and a test or an embedder does.
//
// **It swaps THIS THREAD'S pointer**, `the_vm` being a thread-local, so two threads each entering a
// VM of their own run both at once and neither can see the other's. Nesting on one thread is
// unchanged: each level holds the value it was answered.
enter(vm: *Vm) -> *Vm
    val was = the_vm

    the_vm = vm
    was

// Put back the VM `enter` answered.
leave(was: *Vm)
    the_vm = was

// Give a VM's heap back, run every finalizer still outstanding, and free the block.
//
// **The finalizers run first and they touch no VM state**, each one giving back a sysl `string`, a
// `Buf` or a `Map` the object held -- so this is safe to call for a VM that is not the current one,
// which is what a caller disposing a second VM is doing.
dispose_vm(v: *Vm)
    gc.dispose(&v.heap)
    free(v.region)

    v.region = null
    v.heap_ceiling = 0
    v.heap = gc.heap(null, 0)
